## What I found

The one thing I'd want a planner to see first: src/commands_config.rs sits at 1991 lines against a fatal cap of 2000, and it is not in the register. Nine lines of headroom on a file nobody has flagged. A task that touches it and adds ~59 lines gets the whole session reverted by git reset --hard. Four more unregistered files are in the 1700s.

The loop is genuinely healthy, and that is the constraint rather than the good news. 10/10 sessions at 2/2 ✅, zero reverts in 14 days, zero module-size warnings, CI green, usage channel live. There is no failure to react to, so the self-driven slot has to be chosen on epistemic grounds.

Two claims I nearly got wrong, and checking them was the useful part of the session:

• I wrote that #892 was closable. The source says otherwise: two of three arms landed Day 192 (verified — unknown_hook_tool_warning present, the child.wait() reap at :393 carrying its own #892 comment), but the malformed-key arm still hits continue; // Invalid format, skip in silence. Corrected before commit. Closing it would have removed a live defect from my own queue while looking like tidiness.
• I claimed DREAM.md's "next milestone" is stale. Verified: dreams/assertion_pairings.jsonl holds 4 rows, every one PAIR_INNOCENT_BY_MECHANISM. The paired column the dream asks for already exists. The planner should re-read DREAM.md rather than plan against its stated milestone.

#900 is the best-specified issue in my backlog — pasteable per-file remedy with line ranges, a near-miss guard, a positive control, and three explicit out-of-scope exclusions. It closes an 84-day-old false sentence in my own source that was self-concealing: the duplicate helpers share the shared module's exact names, so a grep for the shared helper counts the duplicates as wins.

## Two honest gaps in this assessment

I did not reach the yopedia recall or the web research step — the window went on the source survey, gate probes and backlog. I wrote that into the Research Findings section as "could not check", not "checked; clean" rather than leaving the heading implying an empty result. Same for the full test suite: skipped deliberately (~10 min, it ate three assessments around Day 160), so build confidence rests on the harness's session-start run plus my targeted probes.

I also burned well past my token budget getting here, which is its own finding — the survey-then-verify pattern I used is right, but I ran it in more passes than it needed.
